1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,820
He shot down 94 Soviet Il-2 ground-attack aircraft, and in the end the rear gunner of an Il-2 hit him.

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,420
His name was Otto Kittel.

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:16,800
He was born in 1917 in Kronsdorf in the Sudetenland, the son of a farmer, and worked briefly as a car mechanic.

4
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:19,920
In 1939 he joined the Luftwaffe.

5
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:26,920
In February 1941 he joined Fighter Wing 54, which was deployed on the Eastern Front before Leningrad.

6
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:35,200
On 19 February 1943 he scored his 39th victory, which was also the 4,000th of his wing.

7
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:40,060
Four weeks later the engine of his Focke-Wulf 190 failed during a mission.

8
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:44,020
He had to make a forced landing about 60 kilometres behind the Soviet front.

9
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:50,400
He made his way back on foot, across frozen Lake Ilmen, and reached the German lines after about three days.

10
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:52,800
After that his score rose quickly.

11
00:00:52,920 --> 00:01:02,560
In September 1943 he scored his 100th victory, in October he received the Knight's Cross, and in April 1944 the Oak Leaves.

12
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:08,340
In November 1944, as a first lieutenant and squadron leader, he was awarded the Swords.

13
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:13,320
By then his wing was in the Courland Pocket in Latvia, cut off from the Reich.

14
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:18,080
In February 1945 he attacked a group of Il-2s at low level.

15
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:22,520
His aircraft was hit by defensive fire and crashed near Džūkste.

16
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:24,580
No German witness saw the crash.

17
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:32,020
He was credited with 267 victories, all on the Eastern Front, in 583 missions.

18
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,680
He was the most successful German fighter pilot to be killed in action.

19
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:38,400
Otto Kittel was 27 years old.
